// SL-221: a parameter whose DEFAULT VALUE suspends may be USED but not OMITTED
// at a call the coroutine transform builds a frame for. This is the same-module
// half; `coro_suspending_default_omitted_cross_module.saw` is the cross-module
// half, and the two assert the SAME sentence — which is the claim, since the
// point of SL-208 is that a module boundary stops changing the answer.
//
// An omitted default is MATERIALIZED at the call site: the callee's declared
// expression is copied in where the author would have written the argument, and
// the frame is built from a full-arity list. A suspending expression cannot be
// materialized that way — it is not a value the site computes, it is a
// suspension, and it would need an embedded sub-frame of its own at every call
// that omits it, with the caller's state split around it. That is a feature, not
// a gap in the filling, so the refusal names the two spellings that work.
//
// NOTHING THAT WORKED IS LOST. On base 7467b1f2 the three spellings of this were
// an arity error (`__saw_drive_target takes 1 argument(s), but 0 were given`), a
// compiler traceback (`IndexError: list index out of range` when embedded), and
// — cross-module — a MISCOMPILE that printed the right answer with
// `__saw_drive_steps` reporting 0 Pending resumes, the SL-208 wedge itself. One
// refusal at the user's own line replaces all three.
//
// Supplying the argument is untouched and is pinned by
// `coro_omitted_default_arguments.saw`, at every position and in both modules.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: the default value of parameter `x` of `target` suspends

import std.task.{yield_now}

func seed() -> Int {
    yield_now()
    7
}

func target(x: Int = seed()) -> Int {
    yield_now()
    x
}

// The EMBEDDED position: a nested call whose callee frame is built by hand.
func root() -> Int {
    target()
}

func main() {
    print(__saw_drive(root()))
}
